window.EVERYO_DOC_INDEX = [{"id":"install","href":"documentation.html#install","title":"Install from source.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"Start with Python 3.9–3.12 and an isolated virtual environment. NumPy and Matplotlib power the core CPU workflow; no account or API key is required.","text":"01 / getting started install from source. start with python 3.9–3.12 and an isolated virtual environment. numpy and matplotlib power the core cpu workflow; no account or api key is required. macos / linux windows terminal · bash / zsh copy code git clone https://github.com/krishanth7/everyo.git cd everyo python -m venv .venv source .venv/bin/activate python -m pip install -e . everyo doctor everyo demo terminal · powershell copy code git clone https://github.com/krishanth7/everyo.git cd everyo python -m venv .venv .venv\\scripts\\activate.ps1 python -m pip install -e . everyo doctor everyo demo your first success check everyo doctor runs a real gradient check and reports optional components. everyo demo trains a digit classifier. installation may download dependencies; core experiments run locally once installed. do i need tensorflow, onnx, or cuda? no. start with the numpy cpu core. optional integrations have their own prerequisites. terminal copy code python -m pip install -e \".[onnx]\" # optional numerical reference: python -m pip install -e \".[tensorflow]\" cuda requires compatible hardware and a separate native extension build. follow the cuda setup guide . gpu runtime tests are not validated in project ci."},{"id":"tensors","href":"documentation.html#tensors","title":"Think in tensors.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"A tensor holds numerical data, its shape, and gradient state. Python lists use float32 by default; NumPy arrays retain their precision.","text":"02 / the numerical foundation think in tensors. a tensor holds numerical data, its shape, and gradient state. python lists use float32 by default; numpy arrays retain their precision. tensor_basics.py copy code import everyo as eo x = eo.tensor([[1.0, 2.0]]) y = eo.tensor([[3.0], [4.0]]) print(x.shape) # (1, 2) print(eo.matmul(x, y)) # tensor([[11.]], shape=(1, 1), dtype=float32) shape the size of every dimension. dtype the numerical precision. requires_grad whether operations build a gradient graph. explore the tensor api"},{"id":"gradients","href":"documentation.html#gradients","title":"Meet your autograd engine.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"EveryO records the multiplication because x requires gradients. The backward pass fills x.grad with [4.] : the derivative of x² at x = 2.","text":"03 / follow the graph meet your autograd engine. everyo records the multiplication because x requires gradients. the backward pass fills x.grad with [4.] : the derivative of x² at x = 2. first_gradient.py copy code import everyo as eo x = eo.tensor([2.0], requires_grad=true) y = x * x y.backward() print(x.grad) # [4.] understand graph traversal and accumulation"},{"id":"network","href":"documentation.html#network","title":"A network you can inspect.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"Connect a four-feature input to a three-class output with a hidden layer and ReLU activation. This model has 131 trainable parameters.","text":"04 / compose layers a network you can inspect. connect a four-feature input to a three-class output with a hidden layer and relu activation. this model has 131 trainable parameters. first_network.py copy code import everyo as eo model = eo.sequential( eo.linear(4, 16, seed=0), eo.relu(), eo.linear(16, 3, seed=1), ) print(model.num_parameters()) # 131 keep the output as logits. crossentropyloss applies log-softmax internally. end your classifier with a plain linear layer."},{"id":"training","href":"documentation.html#training","title":"Generate data. Train. Evaluate.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"This self-contained example creates a seeded dataset, holds out 20% for evaluation, and trains with Adam. Run it after installing EveryO.","text":"05 / a complete experiment generate data. train. evaluate. this self-contained example creates a seeded dataset, holds out 20% for evaluation, and trains with adam. run it after installing everyo. train_classifier.py copy code import everyo as eo from everyo.datasets import make_blobs features, labels = make_blobs( n_samples=600, n_features=4, centers=3, seed=0 ) x_train, x_test, y_train, y_test = eo.stratified_split( features, labels, test_size=0.2, seed=0 ) model = eo.sequential( eo.linear(4, 16, seed=0), eo.relu(), eo.linear(16, 3, seed=1), ) trainer = eo.trainer( model, eo.adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.01), eo.crossentropyloss(), metrics=[\"accuracy\"], ) train_loader = eo.dataloader( eo.arraydataset(x_train, y_train), batch_size=32, shuffle=true, seed=0, ) test_loader = eo.dataloader( eo.arraydataset(x_test, y_test), batch_size=64, ) history = trainer.fit( train_loader, epochs=20, validation_loader=test_loader ) print(trainer.evaluate(test_loader)) accuracy depends on the generated data and training run. inspect the returned evaluation metrics rather than assuming a fixed result."},{"id":"serialization","href":"documentation.html#serialization","title":"Save the model. Keep the experiment.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"EveryO stores model architecture and weights in a pickle-free .evo archive. After training the model above, save it and reload it for inference.","text":"06 / keep your work save the model. keep the experiment. everyo stores model architecture and weights in a pickle-free .evo archive. after training the model above, save it and reload it for inference. continue train_classifier.py copy code from pathlib import path path(\"artifacts\").mkdir(exist_ok=true) eo.save(model, \"artifacts/model.evo\") restored = eo.load(\"artifacts/model.evo\") restored.eval() with eo.no_grad(): predictions = restored(eo.tensor(x_test[:3])) print(predictions.shape) # (3, 3) for supported onnx exports, validate the exported graph in your target runtime. the traced exporter specializes control flow to the recorded execution. see the save/load example"},{"id":"api","href":"documentation.html#api","title":"The essential API.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"Create a tensor. Set requires_grad=True to record operations.","text":"07 / api reference the essential api. eo.tensor(data, ...) create a tensor. set requires_grad=true to record operations. tensor.backward() differentiate a scalar result and accumulate gradients. eo.sequential(*layers) compose layers into a feed-forward model. eo.linear(in_features, out_features) an affine layer with optional bias, initializer, and seed. eo.adam(parameters, lr=...) adaptive optimization with per-parameter state. eo.trainer(model, optimizer, loss_fn) train, evaluate, and predict with callbacks and history. eo.save(model, path) write a pickle-free .evo archive. eo.load(path) rebuild a supported model and restore its parameters. open the complete api map"},{"id":"troubleshooting","href":"documentation.html#troubleshooting","title":"A shorter path to the fix.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"Start with everyo doctor , then check the environment, tensor shapes, and optional integrations.","text":"08 / when something doesn’t click a shorter path to the fix. start with everyo doctor , then check the environment, tensor shapes, and optional integrations. python cannot import everyo activate the same virtual environment used for installation, run python -m pip install -e . from the framework checkout, and use that interpreter to run your script. my model reports a shape mismatch inspect x.shape and the input/output dimensions of each layer. dense layers expect the last dimension to match in_features . images use nhwc: batch, height, width, channels. gradients are accumulating between steps gradient accumulation is intentional. in a manual training loop, call optimizer.zero_grad() before computing the next backward pass. trainer manages the standard training loop. cuda is unavailable the cpu workflow still works. cuda requires the native extension and compatible nvidia hardware. review the cuda guide and optional-component report from everyo doctor . where can i ask for help? use github discussions for usage questions. for a bug, include a minimal example, versions, hardware, and complete error text with private data removed. read the full troubleshooting guide"},{"id":"guides","href":"documentation.html#guides","title":"Go one level deeper.","guide":"Quickstart","excerpt":"Follow the dependency layers from NumPy to Trainer.","text":"09 / keep exploring go one level deeper. guide architecture follow the dependency layers from numpy to trainer. read the guide guide scaling & export understand mixed precision, onnx, and distributed limitations. read the guide help troubleshooting diagnose installation, shapes, devices, and optional components. find help practice runnable examples go from a gradient to convolution and attention. pick an example foundations getting started the complete installation and first-model guide, including tensors, gradients, cnns, charts, and model saving. read the complete guide → internals automatic differentiation trace computation graphs, the reverse-mode backward pass, gradient accumulation, broadcasting, and finite-difference verification. read the complete guide → reference full api reference explore the complete public api: tensors, layers, losses, optimizers, training, callbacks, serialization, profiling, and command-line tools. read the complete guide → advanced cuda & devices build the optional cuda extension, understand fallback and strict mode, inspect kernels, and distinguish host-backed tensors from resident buffers. read the complete guide →"},{"id":"reading-this-guide","href":"docs-getting-started.html#reading-this-guide","title":"Install, understand, and train.","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"Work through the CPU workflow before adding optional integrations. The full upstream guide below includes the first tensor, gradient, neural network, convolution, and saved model.","text":"getting started before you begin install, understand, and train. work through the cpu workflow before adding optional integrations. the full upstream guide below includes the first tensor, gradient, neural network, convolution, and saved model. what should be working when you finish? you should be able to run the installation check, create tensors, inspect a gradient, train and evaluate a small classifier, and save its model. code snippets build on earlier definitions; the overview quickstart also provides a self-contained classifier script."},{"id":"install","href":"docs-getting-started.html#install","title":"Install","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"git clone https://github.com/krishanth7/EveryO.git cd EveryO python -m venv .venv Activate the environment — on Linux/macOS:","text":"getting started install terminal copy code git clone https://github.com/krishanth7/everyo.git cd everyo python -m venv .venv activate the environment — on linux/macos: terminal copy code source .venv/bin/activate on windows (powershell): powershell copy code .venv\\scripts\\activate.ps1 then install everyo in editable mode: terminal copy code pip install -e . check that everything works: terminal copy code everyo info everyo doctor doctor verifies that the numerics are correct (it runs a real gradient check) and reports which optional components are present. neither command sends anything anywhere; everyo collects no telemetry."},{"id":"your-first-tensor","href":"docs-getting-started.html#your-first-tensor","title":"Your first tensor","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"import everyo as eo x = eo.tensor([[1.0, 2.0]]) y = eo.tensor([[3.0], [4.0]]) print(eo.matmul(x, y)) # Tensor([[11.]], shape=(1, 1), dtype=float32) Tensors expose the properties you would expect:","text":"getting started your first tensor python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo x = eo.tensor([[1.0, 2.0]]) y = eo.tensor([[3.0], [4.0]]) print(eo.matmul(x, y)) # tensor([[11.]], shape=(1, 1), dtype=float32) tensors expose the properties you would expect: python · reference snippet copy code x.shape # (1, 2) x.ndim # 2 x.dtype # 'float32' x.device # device('cpu') x.size # 2"},{"id":"gradients","href":"docs-getting-started.html#gradients","title":"Gradients","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"Mark a tensor with requires_grad=True and EveryO records every operation applied to it. Calling .backward() on a scalar result fills in .grad :","text":"getting started gradients mark a tensor with requires_grad=true and everyo records every operation applied to it. calling .backward() on a scalar result fills in .grad : python · reference snippet copy code x = eo.tensor([2.0], requires_grad=true) y = x * x y.backward() print(x.grad) # [4.] because d(x^2)/dx = 2x wrap inference in eo.no_grad() to skip graph construction entirely: python · reference snippet copy code with eo.no_grad(): predictions = model(features)"},{"id":"a-small-network","href":"docs-getting-started.html#a-small-network","title":"A small network","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"The complete installation and first-model guide, including tensors, gradients, CNNs, charts, and model saving.","text":"getting started a small network python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo model = eo.sequential( eo.linear(4, 16), eo.relu(), eo.linear(16, 3), ) print(model.summary())"},{"id":"training-it","href":"docs-getting-started.html#training-it","title":"Training it","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"The complete installation and first-model guide, including tensors, gradients, CNNs, charts, and model saving.","text":"getting started training it python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo from everyo.datasets import make_blobs features, labels = make_blobs(n_samples=600, n_features=4, centers=3, seed=0) x_train, x_test, y_train, y_test = eo.stratified_split(features, labels, test_size=0.2, seed=0) model = eo.sequential(eo.linear(4, 16, seed=0), eo.relu(), eo.linear(16, 3, seed=1)) trainer = eo.trainer( model=model, optimizer=eo.adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.01), loss_fn=eo.crossentropyloss(), metrics=[\"accuracy\"], ) history = trainer.fit( eo.dataloader(eo.arraydataset(x_train, y_train), batch_size=32, shuffle=true, seed=0), epochs=20, validation_loader=eo.dataloader(eo.arraydataset(x_test, y_test), batch_size=64), ) print(trainer.evaluate(eo.dataloader(eo.arraydataset(x_test, y_test), batch_size=64)))"},{"id":"a-convolutional-network","href":"docs-getting-started.html#a-convolutional-network","title":"A convolutional network","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"Images are NHWC — (batch, height, width, channels) — so a single-channel 8x8 image batch has shape (n, 8, 8, 1) :","text":"getting started a convolutional network images are nhwc — (batch, height, width, channels) — so a single-channel 8x8 image batch has shape (n, 8, 8, 1) : this separate architecture is named cnn so the trained model above remains available for evaluation and saving. python · reference snippet copy code cnn = eo.sequential( eo.conv2d(1, 8, 3, padding=\"same\"), eo.relu(), eo.maxpool2d(2), eo.conv2d(8, 16, 3, padding=\"same\"), eo.relu(), eo.maxpool2d(2), eo.flatten(), eo.linear(2 * 2 * 16, 10), ) padding accepts \"valid\" , \"same\" or an int, and stride accepts an int or a (height, width) pair. see examples/convolutional_network.py ."},{"id":"charts-saving-and-loading","href":"docs-getting-started.html#charts-saving-and-loading","title":"Charts, saving and loading","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"from pathlib import Path Path(\"artifacts\").mkdir(exist_ok=True) eo.plot_history(history, save_path=\"artifacts/history.png\") eo.save(model, \"artifacts/model.evo\") restored = eo.load(\"artifacts/model.evo\") Charts work without a display: on a headless machine EveryO selects the non-interactive Agg backend automatically.","text":"getting started charts, saving and loading python · reference snippet copy code from pathlib import path path(\"artifacts\").mkdir(exist_ok=true) eo.plot_history(history, save_path=\"artifacts/history.png\") eo.save(model, \"artifacts/model.evo\") restored = eo.load(\"artifacts/model.evo\") charts work without a display: on a headless machine everyo selects the non-interactive agg backend automatically."},{"id":"where-to-go-next","href":"docs-getting-started.html#where-to-go-next","title":"Where to go next","guide":"Getting started","excerpt":"The complete installation and first-model guide, including tensors, gradients, CNNs, charts, and model saving.","text":"getting started where to go next architecture — how the package fits together. autograd — how the gradient engine works. cuda — building and using the optional gpu kernels. examples/ — runnable scripts covering every feature."},{"id":"reading-this-guide","href":"docs-architecture.html#reading-this-guide","title":"Follow the whole stack.","guide":"Architecture","excerpt":"Read the architecture from NumPy upward. Each layer gives the next one a smaller, clearer contract: storage, differentiable operations, modules, optimization, and training.","text":"architecture before you begin follow the whole stack. read the architecture from numpy upward. each layer gives the next one a smaller, clearer contract: storage, differentiable operations, modules, optimization, and training. 05 training & visualization trainer · callbacks · history · charts ↓ 04 optimization sgd · adam · parameter updates ↓ 03 neural-network modules layers · activations · losses · state ↓ 02 core tensors & autograd data · shapes · graph · derivatives ↓ 01 backend dispatch numpy cpu · optional native cuda data loading feeds the training loop. serialization saves model configuration and parameters. a training step across the layers load a batch. dataloader yields features and targets. run the model. module calls compose differentiable tensor operations. measure the loss. the loss reduces prediction errors to a scalar. propagate gradients. backward() traverses the recorded graph. update parameters. sgd or adam applies the gradients; history records the epoch results. two cuda paths, two contracts the standard tensor architecture described below keeps numpy buffers in host memory. the separate eo.cuda.to_device() resident-buffer api is inference-only and does not attach autograd nodes. see the device guide for that distinction. one update without trainer this complete example makes the training contract explicit. the optimizer receives model parameters, the forward pass creates a scalar loss, backward() fills the gradients, and step() applies one update. python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo features = eo.tensor([[1.0, 2.0]]) targets = eo.tensor([[1.0]]) model = eo.sequential(eo.linear(2, 1, seed=0)) optimizer = eo.sgd(model.parameters(), lr=0.01) criterion = eo.mseloss() model.train() optimizer.zero_grad() predictions = model(features) loss = criterion(predictions, targets) loss.backward() optimizer.step() print(loss.item()) # loss from the forward pass, before the update call zero_grad() before each fresh update. use model.eval() and eo.no_grad() when you want evaluation behavior without recording a new gradient graph. trainer manages the standard epoch loop for you."},{"id":"layers","href":"docs-architecture.html#layers","title":"Layers","guide":"Architecture","excerpt":"The foundation.","text":"architecture layers everyo.core the foundation. dtype.py — the small set of supported numpy dtypes, with float32 as the default floating point type. device.py — the device abstraction. buffers always live in host memory; the device selects where operations execute . requesting cuda without a working extension degrades to cpu with a warning rather than failing. tensor.py — the tensor type: data, device, gradient state, operators. operations.py — every differentiable operation. each one validates its inputs, computes the forward result (dispatching to cuda when the tensor is on a cuda device and a kernel exists) and records a graph node. autograd.py — the reverse-mode engine: graph nodes, an iterative topological sort, gradient accumulation and the no_grad mode flag. convolution.py — convolution and pooling. these live apart from operations.py because they need windowing helpers nothing else uses, and because the file would otherwise dwarf every other module. the forward pass is im2col, which turns a convolution into a single matrix multiplication; the backward pass is two more multiplications plus a scatter-add back into the padded image. everyo.backends operator_backend.py defines the common operatorbackend contract and owns all device dispatch and fallback decisions. numpy_backend.py is the reference implementation of every kernel everyo needs. tensorflow_backend.py is optional and used for cross-checking numerics, for a keras reference model and for benchmarks. importing the package never imports tensorflow. everyo.cuda availability.py answers \"can we run cuda kernels right now?\" without ever raising. interface.py mirrors the numpy kernel signatures and exposes supports() plus unsupported_reason() so dispatch misses are observable and testable. direct calls fall back to numpy unless strict=true is passed. the kernels themselves live in the top-level cuda/ directory and are built separately. everyo.nn module owns parameters and sub-modules, and can describe itself as a plain dictionary via get_config() . that description is what makes pickle-free serialisation possible. layers, activations and losses all build on it. everyo.optim optimizer holds the parameter list and per-parameter state keyed by object identity. sgd and adam implement step() . everyo.data dataset is a two-method contract ( __len__ , __getitem__ ). dataloader batches and shuffles it, with a fast path that slices whole arrays when the dataset is an arraydataset . preprocessing follows a fit / transform split so that scaling statistics come from training data only. everyo.training trainer runs the loop; callback observers can stop training, checkpoint, log or reschedule the learning rate. history records one row per epoch and exports to json or csv. everyo.serialization an .evo file is a zip archive holding manifest.json (metadata plus the architecture as json) and parameters.npz (saved and loaded with allow_pickle=false ). loading rebuilds the model by looking class names up in a registry, so opening an untrusted file cannot execute code."},{"id":"design-decisions","href":"docs-architecture.html#design-decisions","title":"Design decisions","guide":"Architecture","excerpt":"Buffers stay in host memory. A CUDA device tag changes where kernels run , not where data lives. Transfers happen inside each kernel call. This costs bandwidth, and the benchmarks measure that cost honestly; in exchange the data model stays simple enough to read.","text":"architecture design decisions buffers stay in host memory. a cuda device tag changes where kernels run , not where data lives. transfers happen inside each kernel call. this costs bandwidth, and the benchmarks measure that cost honestly; in exchange the data model stays simple enough to read. images are nhwc . (batch, height, width, channels) is tensorflow's native layout, and everyo uses tensorflow as its correctness reference — so convolution results can be compared against tf.nn.conv2d without transposing anything, which is exactly what the test suite does. float32 by default. python lists and scalars become float32 ; a numpy array keeps its own precision, so float64 work stays float64 . gradient checks in the test suite use float64 for accuracy. gradients are recorded only when needed. a node is created only when gradient mode is on and at least one input requires gradients, so inference costs nothing extra. errors name the shapes. everyoshapeerror messages state what was attempted, which shapes were involved and what would have been valid. optional means optional. tensorflow and cuda are detected safely, report why they are unavailable, and never prevent the core from working."},{"id":"extending-everyo","href":"docs-architecture.html#extending-everyo","title":"Extending EveryO","guide":"Architecture","excerpt":"To add a differentiable operation:","text":"architecture extending everyo to add a differentiable operation: implement the forward pass in everyo/core/operations.py . return the gradients for each input from the backward_fn closure. add a finite-difference check to tests/test_autograd.py . to add a layer: subclass module , decorate it with @register_module so it can be serialised, and implement forward . implement get_config() if the constructor takes arguments. export it from everyo/nn/__init__.py and the top-level everyo/__init__.py . add tests, including a save/load round trip."},{"id":"reading-this-guide","href":"docs-autograd.html#reading-this-guide","title":"Make the chain rule concrete.","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"Every differentiable operation computes a result and records how to send a gradient back to its inputs. Follow the forward graph first, then walk the same operations backward.","text":"automatic differentiation before you begin make the chain rule concrete. every differentiable operation computes a result and records how to send a gradient back to its inputs. follow the forward graph first, then walk the same operations backward. work through y = x × x at x = 2 the forward pass reads x twice and produces y = 4. the scalar backward pass starts with an incoming gradient of 1. each multiplication input contributes 1 × 2 to x. the two paths accumulate: x.grad = 2 + 2 = 4. accumulation is part of the contract calling backward() again adds gradients. clear them between optimizer steps unless accumulation is intentional. intermediate tensors keep gradients only when requested with retain_grad(). observe accumulation, then reset it run this complete example after installation. rebuilding the same scalar expression creates a fresh graph, while the leaf tensor keeps its accumulated gradient until you clear it. python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo x = eo.tensor([2.0], requires_grad=true) (x * x).backward() print(x.grad) # [4.] (x * x).backward() print(x.grad) # [8.] -- contributions accumulate x.zero_grad() (x * x).backward() print(x.grad) # [4.]"},{"id":"the-computation-graph","href":"docs-autograd.html#the-computation-graph","title":"The computation graph","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"Every differentiable operation does three things:","text":"automatic differentiation the computation graph every differentiable operation does three things: computes the forward result, captures whatever it needs for the backward pass in a closure, attaches a node recording the inputs and that closure. python · reference snippet copy code def multiply(a, b): left, right = _binary_inputs(a, b, \"multiply\") data = _dispatch(\"multiply\", device, left.data, right.data) def backward_fn(gradient): return ( unbroadcast(gradient * right.data, left.shape), unbroadcast(gradient * left.data, right.shape), ) return _make(data, (left, right), \"multiply\", backward_fn) the closure implements the product rule: d(ab)/da = b and d(ab)/db = a , each multiplied by the incoming gradient (the chain rule). nodes are created only when gradient mode is enabled and at least one input requires gradients. inference therefore builds no graph at all."},{"id":"the-backward-pass","href":"docs-autograd.html#the-backward-pass","title":"The backward pass","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"Tensor.backward() seeds the output gradient (ones, for a scalar), walks the graph in reverse topological order and accumulates gradients into each tensor's .grad .","text":"automatic differentiation the backward pass tensor.backward() seeds the output gradient (ones, for a scalar), walks the graph in reverse topological order and accumulates gradients into each tensor's .grad . python · reference snippet copy code for node in topological_order(root): incoming = gradients.get(id(node)) if node.requires_grad and (node.is_leaf or node.retains_grad): node.accumulate_grad(incoming) for parent, parent_grad in zip(node.grad_node.parents, node.grad_node.backward_fn(incoming)): gradients[id(parent)] = gradients.get(id(parent), 0) + parent_grad two details matter: the traversal is iterative. a recursive walk would hit python's recursion limit on deep graphs; tests/test_autograd.py checks a 2000-operation chain. gradients are summed, not replaced. when a tensor feeds several operations, every path contributes. that is why x + x gives dx = 2 , and why you must call zero_grad() between steps unless you want accumulation."},{"id":"broadcasting","href":"docs-autograd.html#broadcasting","title":"Broadcasting","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"NumPy broadcasting means a (3,) bias can be added to a (32, 3) activation. The gradient then arrives with the broadcast shape (32, 3) and must be summed back down to (3,) — summing over the expanded axes is exactly the derivative of broadcasting. unbroadcast() does this, and every broadcasting operation routes its gradients through it.","text":"automatic differentiation broadcasting numpy broadcasting means a (3,) bias can be added to a (32, 3) activation. the gradient then arrives with the broadcast shape (32, 3) and must be summed back down to (3,) — summing over the expanded axes is exactly the derivative of broadcasting. unbroadcast() does this, and every broadcasting operation routes its gradients through it."},{"id":"only-leaves-keep-gradients","href":"docs-autograd.html#only-leaves-keep-gradients","title":"Only leaves keep gradients","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"Intermediate tensors do not retain .grad , which keeps memory bounded. Call .retain_grad() on one if you want to inspect it:","text":"automatic differentiation only leaves keep gradients intermediate tensors do not retain .grad , which keeps memory bounded. call .retain_grad() on one if you want to inspect it: python · reference snippet copy code hidden = (x * 3).retain_grad() (hidden * 2).backward() print(hidden.grad) # [2.]"},{"id":"turning-it-off","href":"docs-autograd.html#turning-it-off","title":"Turning it off","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"with eo.no_grad(): predictions = model(features) # no graph is built no_grad is thread-local, works as a decorator, and restores the previous mode on exit. enable_grad() re-enables tracking inside a no_grad block.","text":"automatic differentiation turning it off python · reference snippet copy code with eo.no_grad(): predictions = model(features) # no graph is built no_grad is thread-local, works as a decorator, and restores the previous mode on exit. enable_grad() re-enables tracking inside a no_grad block."},{"id":"supported-operations","href":"docs-autograd.html#supported-operations","title":"Supported operations","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"Gradients are implemented and finite-difference tested for: add, subtract, multiply, divide, negate, power, exp, log, sqrt, abs, clip, matmul (including the vector cases), dot, sum, mean, max, min, variance, reshape, transpose, flatten, concatenate, stack, indexing, ReLU, sigmoid, tanh, softmax and log-softmax.","text":"automatic differentiation supported operations gradients are implemented and finite-difference tested for: add, subtract, multiply, divide, negate, power, exp, log, sqrt, abs, clip, matmul (including the vector cases), dot, sum, mean, max, min, variance, reshape, transpose, flatten, concatenate, stack, indexing, relu, sigmoid, tanh, softmax and log-softmax."},{"id":"how-correctness-is-verified","href":"docs-autograd.html#how-correctness-is-verified","title":"How correctness is verified","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"For a scalar function f , the central difference","text":"automatic differentiation how correctness is verified for a scalar function f , the central difference reference / output copy code (f(x + h) - f(x - h)) / 2h approximates df/dx with error o(h²). tests/test_autograd.py compares every analytic gradient against this approximation in float64 . if a backward pass were wrong, the comparison would fail — which is exactly what happened, and got caught, while this engine was being written."},{"id":"what-is-not-implemented-in-v0-1-0","href":"docs-autograd.html#what-is-not-implemented-in-v0-1-0","title":"What is not implemented in v0.1.0","guide":"Automatic differentiation","excerpt":"Trace computation graphs, the reverse-mode backward pass, gradient accumulation, broadcasting, and finite-difference verification.","text":"automatic differentiation what is not implemented in v0.1.0 second-order derivatives (the graph is not differentiable itself). in-place operations that would invalidate saved tensors. gradients through integer or boolean tensors (these cannot require grad)."},{"id":"reading-this-guide","href":"docs-api-reference.html#reading-this-guide","title":"Find the API. Understand its role.","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"This is the complete upstream API map. Entries identify public names and important arguments; source docstrings remain the authoritative field-by-field reference.","text":"full api reference before you begin find the api. understand its role. this is the complete upstream api map. entries identify public names and important arguments; source docstrings remain the authoritative field-by-field reference. how to read a signature eo. imported as import everyo as eo ; the api map lists top-level exports. * arguments after this marker are keyword-only. ... additional arguments exist. use the source docstring for the full contract. import paths matter specialized apis also live under everyo.quantization , everyo.profiler , and everyo.distributed_tcp . the shared-memory everyo.distributed api is the single-machine path described below; tcp groups are a separate api."},{"id":"tensors","href":"docs-api-reference.html#tensors","title":"Tensors","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"Tensor properties: .shape , .ndim , .size , .dtype , .device , .requires_grad , .is_leaf , .grad , .T .","text":"full api reference tensors name purpose eo.tensor(data, *, dtype, device, requires_grad) create a tensor eo.as_tensor(data) convert only when needed eo.is_tensor(obj) type check eo.zeros , eo.ones , eo.full , eo.eye filled tensors eo.zeros_like , eo.ones_like match an existing tensor eo.arange , eo.linspace ranges eo.uniform , eo.normal , eo.rand , eo.randn random tensors (accept seed= ) eo.one_hot(indices, num_classes) one-hot encoding tensor properties: .shape , .ndim , .size , .dtype , .device , .requires_grad , .is_leaf , .grad , .t . tensor methods: .backward() , .zero_grad() , .retain_grad() , .detach() , .numpy() , .tolist() , .item() , .astype() , .to() , .cpu() , .cuda() , .clone() , .reshape() , .transpose() , .flatten() , .sum() , .mean() , .max() , .min() , .argmax() , .argmin() , .exp() , .log() , .sqrt() , .abs() , .clip() , .matmul() , .dot() ."},{"id":"operations","href":"docs-api-reference.html#operations","title":"Operations","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"Arithmetic: add , subtract , multiply , divide , negative , power , exp , log , sqrt , abs , clip .","text":"full api reference operations arithmetic: add , subtract , multiply , divide , negative , power , exp , log , sqrt , abs , clip . linear algebra: matmul , dot . reductions: sum , mean , max , min , variance . shape: reshape , transpose , flatten , concatenate , stack . activations: relu , sigmoid , tanh , softmax , log_softmax . convolution: conv2d(x, weight, bias, *, stride, padding) , max_pool2d(x, pool_size, *, stride, padding) , avg_pool2d(x, pool_size, *, stride, padding) . images are nhwc ( batch, height, width, channels ) and kernels are (kernel_h, kernel_w, in_channels, out_channels) — tensorflow's layout. padding is \"valid\" , \"same\" or an int."},{"id":"gradient-mode","href":"docs-api-reference.html#gradient-mode","title":"Gradient mode","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.no_grad() , eo.enable_grad() , eo.set_grad_enabled(mode) — all usable as context managers or decorators.","text":"full api reference gradient mode eo.no_grad() , eo.enable_grad() , eo.set_grad_enabled(mode) — all usable as context managers or decorators."},{"id":"layers-and-models","href":"docs-api-reference.html#layers-and-models","title":"Layers and models","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"Attention helpers: eo.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, *, mask, dropout) , eo.causal_mask(size) , eo.padding_mask(lengths, size) .","text":"full api reference layers and models name purpose eo.module base class for everything with parameters eo.parameter a tensor an optimizer updates eo.sequential(*layers) chain layers eo.linear(in_features, out_features, *, bias, initializer, seed) affine layer eo.conv2d(in_channels, out_channels, kernel_size, *, stride, padding, bias, initializer, seed) 2-d convolution over nhwc images eo.maxpool2d(pool_size, *, stride, padding) max pooling eo.avgpool2d(pool_size, *, stride, padding) average pooling eo.embedding(num_embeddings, embedding_dim, *, initializer, seed) learned id-to-vector lookup eo.batchnorm1d(num_features, ...) / eo.batchnorm2d(...) normalise per feature across the batch eo.layernorm(normalized_shape, ...) normalise each sample across its own features eo.rnn / eo.lstm / eo.gru (input_size, hidden_size, *, return_sequences, ...) recurrent layers over (batch, time, features) eo.multiheadattention(embed_dim, num_heads, *, dropout, bias, seed) self- or cross-attention eo.positionalencoding(embed_dim, *, max_length, dropout) fixed sinusoidal positions eo.transformerencoderblock(embed_dim, num_heads, ...) attention + feed-forward with residuals eo.transformerencoder(embed_dim, num_heads, *, num_layers, ...) a stack of encoder blocks eo.flatten(start_dim=1) collapse trailing dimensions eo.dropout(p, *, seed) inverted dropout eo.relu , eo.sigmoid , eo.tanh , eo.softmax , eo.logsoftmax activation modules attention helpers: eo.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, *, mask, dropout) , eo.causal_mask(size) , eo.padding_mask(lengths, size) . module methods: parameters() , named_parameters() , named_modules() , register_buffer() , named_buffers() , buffers() , num_parameters() , zero_grad() , train() , eval() , to(device) , state_dict() , load_state_dict() , get_config() , summary() ."},{"id":"losses","href":"docs-api-reference.html#losses","title":"Losses","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.MSELoss , eo.MAELoss , eo.BCELoss , eo.BCEWithLogitsLoss , eo.CrossEntropyLoss — all accept reduction in {\"mean\", \"sum\", \"none\"} . Functional forms live in everyo.nn : mse_loss , mae_loss , binary_cross_entropy , cross_entropy .","text":"full api reference losses eo.mseloss , eo.maeloss , eo.bceloss , eo.bcewithlogitsloss , eo.crossentropyloss — all accept reduction in {\"mean\", \"sum\", \"none\"} . functional forms live in everyo.nn : mse_loss , mae_loss , binary_cross_entropy , cross_entropy . crossentropyloss takes raw logits and applies log-softmax internally, so the final layer should be a plain linear ."},{"id":"optimizers","href":"docs-api-reference.html#optimizers","title":"Optimizers","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"Both provide zero_grad() , step() and get_config() .","text":"full api reference optimizers name arguments eo.sgd lr , momentum , weight_decay , nesterov eo.adam lr , betas , eps , weight_decay , amsgrad both provide zero_grad() , step() and get_config() ."},{"id":"data","href":"docs-api-reference.html#data","title":"Data","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.Dataset , eo.ArrayDataset , eo.TransformDataset , eo.Subset , eo.load_csv_dataset , eo.DataLoader .","text":"full api reference data eo.dataset , eo.arraydataset , eo.transformdataset , eo.subset , eo.load_csv_dataset , eo.dataloader . preprocessing: eo.standardscaler , eo.minmaxscaler , eo.standardize , eo.one_hot_encode ; everyo.data additionally exports normalize , min_max_scale and shuffle_arrays . splitting: eo.train_test_split , eo.random_split , eo.stratified_split ."},{"id":"training","href":"docs-api-reference.html#training","title":"Training","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.Trainer(model, optimizer, loss_fn, *, metrics, device, gradient_clip) with fit() , evaluate() , predict() and predict_classes() .","text":"full api reference training eo.trainer(model, optimizer, loss_fn, *, metrics, device, gradient_clip) with fit() , evaluate() , predict() and predict_classes() . callbacks: eo.earlystopping , eo.modelcheckpoint , eo.progresslogger , eo.csvlogger , eo.learningratescheduler , and eo.callback to write your own. metrics: eo.accuracy , eo.confusion_matrix ; everyo.training also exports binary_accuracy , mean_absolute_error , mean_squared_error and r2_score . eo.history supports history[\"loss\"] , .best() , .last() , .to_dict() , .to_json() and .to_csv() ."},{"id":"serialization","href":"docs-api-reference.html#serialization","title":"Serialization","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.save(model, path, *, metadata, overwrite) , eo.load(path, *, model, strict) and eo.inspect_archive(path) .","text":"full api reference serialization eo.save(model, path, *, metadata, overwrite) , eo.load(path, *, model, strict) and eo.inspect_archive(path) ."},{"id":"visualization","href":"docs-api-reference.html#visualization","title":"Visualization","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.plot_loss , eo.plot_accuracy , eo.plot_history , eo.plot_confusion_matrix , eo.plot_predictions , eo.plot_benchmark ; everyo.visualization also exports plot_metric , plot_decision_boundary and plot_benchmark_bars . All accept save_path= and work headlessly.","text":"full api reference visualization eo.plot_loss , eo.plot_accuracy , eo.plot_history , eo.plot_confusion_matrix , eo.plot_predictions , eo.plot_benchmark ; everyo.visualization also exports plot_metric , plot_decision_boundary and plot_benchmark_bars . all accept save_path= and work headlessly."},{"id":"devices-and-cuda","href":"docs-api-reference.html#devices-and-cuda","title":"Devices and CUDA","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.device(spec, *, strict) , eo.Device , and the eo.cuda namespace: is_available() , device_count() , runtime_info() , unavailable_reason() , plus the kernels add , multiply , matmul , relu , sum_all (each taking strict= ).","text":"full api reference devices and cuda eo.device(spec, *, strict) , eo.device , and the eo.cuda namespace: is_available() , device_count() , runtime_info() , unavailable_reason() , plus the kernels add , multiply , matmul , relu , sum_all (each taking strict= )."},{"id":"datasets","href":"docs-api-reference.html#datasets","title":"Datasets","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"everyo.datasets : load_digits , render_digit , make_regression , make_blobs , make_moons , make_spirals , make_xor . All are generated locally from a seed; nothing is downloaded.","text":"full api reference datasets everyo.datasets : load_digits , render_digit , make_regression , make_blobs , make_moons , make_spirals , make_xor . all are generated locally from a seed; nothing is downloaded."},{"id":"configuration-and-logging","href":"docs-api-reference.html#configuration-and-logging","title":"Configuration and logging","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.load_config(path) , eo.Config , eo.ModelConfig , eo.TrainingConfig , eo.DeviceConfig ; eo.configure_logging(level) and eo.get_logger(name) .","text":"full api reference configuration and logging eo.load_config(path) , eo.config , eo.modelconfig , eo.trainingconfig , eo.deviceconfig ; eo.configure_logging(level) and eo.get_logger(name) ."},{"id":"mixed-precision","href":"docs-api-reference.html#mixed-precision","title":"Mixed precision","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.autocast(enabled=True, *, dtype=\"float16\") — context manager and decorator; eo.is_autocast_enabled() , eo.autocast_dtype() . eo.GradScaler(init_scale, *, growth_factor, backoff_factor, growth_interval, enabled) with scale , backward , unscale_ , step , update , get_scale , state_dict and load_state_dict . Only matmul and conv2d are autocast; see scaling.md .","text":"full api reference mixed precision eo.autocast(enabled=true, *, dtype=\"float16\") — context manager and decorator; eo.is_autocast_enabled() , eo.autocast_dtype() . eo.gradscaler(init_scale, *, growth_factor, backoff_factor, growth_interval, enabled) with scale , backward , unscale_ , step , update , get_scale , state_dict and load_state_dict . only matmul and conv2d are autocast; see scaling.md ."},{"id":"onnx-export","href":"docs-api-reference.html#onnx-export","title":"ONNX export","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"eo.export_onnx(model, path, *, input_shape, input_name, output_name, opset, model_name) , eo.run_onnx(path, inputs, *, input_name) , eo.onnx_available() . everyo.serialization.onnx_export also exports SUPPORTED_LAYERS and DEFAULT_OPSET . Needs the optional everyo[onnx] extra.","text":"full api reference onnx export eo.export_onnx(model, path, *, input_shape, input_name, output_name, opset, model_name) , eo.run_onnx(path, inputs, *, input_name) , eo.onnx_available() . everyo.serialization.onnx_export also exports supported_layers and default_opset . needs the optional everyo[onnx] extra."},{"id":"distributed-training","href":"docs-api-reference.html#distributed-training","title":"Distributed training","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"everyo.distributed : spawn(fn, world_size, *, args, kwargs, start_method, timeout) , ProcessGroup ( rank , world_size , is_main , barrier , all_reduce_mean , broadcast , close ), average_gradients(parameters, group) , all_reduce_mean(arrays, group) , shard_indices(count, rank, world_size, *, drop_last) , available_workers() , DistributedError . Single machine only.","text":"full api reference distributed training everyo.distributed : spawn(fn, world_size, *, args, kwargs, start_method, timeout) , processgroup ( rank , world_size , is_main , barrier , all_reduce_mean , broadcast , close ), average_gradients(parameters, group) , all_reduce_mean(arrays, group) , shard_indices(count, rank, world_size, *, drop_last) , available_workers() , distributederror . single machine only."},{"id":"exceptions","href":"docs-api-reference.html#exceptions","title":"Exceptions","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"EveryOError is the base class. Subclasses: EveryOShapeError , EveryODeviceError , EveryODTypeError , EveryOGradientError , EveryOSerializationError , EveryOConfigurationError , EveryOBackendError , EveryOCudaError , and everyo.distributed.DistributedError .","text":"full api reference exceptions everyoerror is the base class. subclasses: everyoshapeerror , everyodeviceerror , everyodtypeerror , everyogradienterror , everyoserializationerror , everyoconfigurationerror , everyobackenderror , everyocudaerror , and everyo.distributed.distributederror ."},{"id":"quantization","href":"docs-api-reference.html#quantization","title":"Quantization","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"everyo.quantization.quantize(array, axis=None) creates a symmetric int8 representation. quantize_dynamic(model) returns an inference-only copy where each Linear layer is replaced by a per-output-channel QuantizedLinear . Activations stay floating point; no calibration dataset is required.","text":"full api reference quantization everyo.quantization.quantize(array, axis=none) creates a symmetric int8 representation. quantize_dynamic(model) returns an inference-only copy where each linear layer is replaced by a per-output-channel quantizedlinear . activations stay floating point; no calibration dataset is required."},{"id":"profiling","href":"docs-api-reference.html#profiling","title":"Profiling","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"Use with everyo.profiler.profile() as result: around inference or training. Every module call is timed only while the context is active. summary() aggregates calls, while export_chrome_trace() produces a trace for Perfetto or Chrome DevTools. record_function() adds user-defined regions.","text":"full api reference profiling use with everyo.profiler.profile() as result: around inference or training. every module call is timed only while the context is active. summary() aggregates calls, while export_chrome_trace() produces a trace for perfetto or chrome devtools. record_function() adds user-defined regions."},{"id":"command-line","href":"docs-api-reference.html#command-line","title":"Command line","guide":"Full API reference","excerpt":"Explore the complete public API: tensors, layers, losses, optimizers, training, callbacks, serialization, profiling, and command-line tools.","text":"full api reference command line reference / output copy code everyo info # versions and available backends everyo doctor # diagnose the installation everyo benchmark # measure matmul across backends everyo test # run the bundled test suite everyo demo # train the bundled digit classifier"},{"id":"reading-this-guide","href":"docs-scaling.html#reading-this-guide","title":"Scale the idea with context.","guide":"Scaling & export","excerpt":"Treat precision, export, and parallel execution as separate choices. Each has distinct prerequisites, numerical checks, and deployment boundaries.","text":"scaling & export before you begin scale the idea with context. treat precision, export, and parallel execution as separate choices. each has distinct prerequisites, numerical checks, and deployment boundaries. read the validation boundary first the shared-memory process groups in this guide are single-machine. the later tcp process-group api is separate and has been exercised across processes, not separate physical hosts. cuda runtime behavior needs hardware validation; cpu stand-ins only check the resident-buffer transfer design. choose one change to validate mixed precision: check numerical behavior and which operations autocast. onnx: check supported operations, input shapes, and agreement in your target runtime. parallelism: control blas threads, keep collective order consistent, and compare with a one-process baseline."},{"id":"mixed-precision","href":"docs-scaling.html#mixed-precision","title":"Mixed precision","guide":"Scaling & export","excerpt":"Inside an eo.autocast() block, exactly two operations change precision: matmul and conv2d . Everything else — reductions, exponentials, normalization statistics, losses — stays in float32, because summing thousands of values or exponentiating one is where 16 bits runs out of range.","text":"scaling & export mixed precision what autocast changes inside an eo.autocast() block, exactly two operations change precision: matmul and conv2d . everything else — reductions, exponentials, normalization statistics, losses — stays in float32, because summing thousands of values or exponentiating one is where 16 bits runs out of range. python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo model = eo.sequential(eo.linear(8, 4, seed=0), eo.relu(), eo.linear(4, 1, seed=1)) with eo.autocast(): output = model(eo.ones(3, 8)) output.dtype # 'float16' model[0].weight.dtype # 'float32' the mechanism is a cast node, not a flag. when autocast is on, each floating operand of an allowlisted operation is routed through a differentiable cast; the forward pass then runs in float16, and the cast's backward pass converts the incoming gradient back to the operand's own dtype. a float32 parameter therefore keeps a float32 gradient — the \"fp32 master weights\" arrangement — while activations, and the gradients flowing through them, genuinely stay in float16. that last part matters more than it sounds. if the backward pass quietly ran in float32, nothing would ever underflow, and loss scaling would be theatre. autocast also works as a decorator and nests: python · reference snippet copy code @eo.autocast() def forward(model, batch): return model(batch) with eo.autocast(): with eo.autocast(false): # a float32 island inside a float16 block logits = sensitive_head(features) why gradscaler exists float16's smallest normal value is about 6.1e-5 and its smallest subnormal about 6e-8 . real gradients are routinely below both, so they flush to zero and the weights never move. gradscaler multiplies the loss by a large constant before backward() , which shifts the whole gradient distribution into representable range, and divides it back out before the optimizer steps. measured on a small mlp whose loss has been scaled down into that regime: reference / output copy code gradient elements flushed to zero (193 total) no gradscaler ........ 192 / 193 with gradscaler ...... 1 / 193 final mse after 400 steps autocast, no scaler .. 6.2983 autocast + scaler .... 0.0474 float32 reference: 0.0474 the scale is chosen dynamically: it grows after growth_interval successful steps and halves the moment a gradient overflows to infinity — and that step is skipped rather than applied, because an infinite gradient would destroy the weights. python · reference snippet copy code scaler = eo.gradscaler() for batch, targets in loader: optimizer.zero_grad() with eo.autocast(): predictions = model(batch) loss = criterion(predictions, targets) scaler.backward(loss) # scale, then backward applied = scaler.step(optimizer) # false when the step was skipped scaler.update() # grow or shrink the scale scaler.backward(loss) is scaler.scale(loss).backward() with numpy's overflow warnings suppressed — an overflow there is the signal step() acts on, not a condition worth printing. use scaler.scale(loss).backward() if you want to see them. scaler.state_dict() and load_state_dict() round-trip the scale, so resuming from a checkpoint does not restart the search. the honest caveat on a cpu, mixed precision saves memory, not time . numpy has no native float16 arithmetic: it upcasts to float32 to compute and casts back, so the float16 path is usually slower here. the speedup mixed precision is known for comes from gpu tensor cores. everyo implements the numerics so the behaviour is right; it does not pretend to make your cpu faster."},{"id":"onnx-export","href":"docs-scaling.html#onnx-export","title":"ONNX export","guide":"Scaling & export","excerpt":"model.eval() eo.export_onnx(model, \"model.onnx\", input_shape=(1, 8, 8, 1), output_name=\"logits\") The batch dimension is exported as a symbolic dimension, so the graph accepts any batch size regardless of what input_shape said.","text":"scaling & export onnx export python · reference snippet copy code model.eval() eo.export_onnx(model, \"model.onnx\", input_shape=(1, 8, 8, 1), output_name=\"logits\") the batch dimension is exported as a symbolic dimension, so the graph accepts any batch size regardless of what input_shape said. supported layers sequential , linear , conv2d , maxpool2d , avgpool2d , flatten , dropout , batchnorm1d , batchnorm2d , layernorm , relu , sigmoid , tanh , softmax , logsoftmax . not supported : rnn , lstm , gru , embedding , multiheadattention , positionalencoding and the transformer blocks. these unroll into long chains of primitives that need a tracing exporter rather than a layer-by-layer one, and a half-correct translation would be worse than none. passing one raises everyoserializationerror naming the layer. two decisions worth knowing layout. everyo uses nhwc ; onnx's conv , maxpool and averagepool are defined on nchw . every convolution and pooling node is therefore wrapped in a pair of real transpose nodes, and the kernel is permuted from (kh, kw, in, out) to onnx's (out, in, kh, kw) . onnx runtime's optimizer usually cancels adjacent transposes, but the exported graph is faithful first and fast second. padding. \"same\" is written out as an explicit pads attribute computed by everyo's own padding resolver, not as auto_pad . tensorflow's \"same\" rule is asymmetric on odd inputs, and auto_pad would not reproduce it exactly. batch normalization is folded into a single scale-and-shift on the channel axis, which is numerically identical to onnx's batchnormalization and sidesteps the layout question entirely. a layer built with track_running_stats=false has no inference-time statistics to export and is refused rather than guessed at. verifying an export python · reference snippet copy code import numpy as np expected = np.asarray(model(eo.tensor(batch)).data) actual = eo.run_onnx(\"model.onnx\", batch) np.abs(expected - actual).max() a trained digit cnn exported and re-run through onnx runtime agrees to 1.1e-05 — float32 rounding from a different order of operations — and every prediction matches. everyo 's own test suite makes this comparison for each supported layer rather than checking that a file was written. both packages are optional: terminal copy code pip install everyo[onnx] eo.onnx_available() reports whether onnx is importable, and run_onnx raises with an install hint when onnxruntime is not."},{"id":"data-parallel-training","href":"docs-scaling.html#data-parallel-training","title":"Data-parallel training","guide":"Scaling & export","excerpt":"Give every worker a copy of the model and a different slice of the batch, then average the gradients before each optimizer step. Averaging over disjoint shards is the same arithmetic as one large batch, so the result matches single-process training.","text":"scaling & export data-parallel training give every worker a copy of the model and a different slice of the batch, then average the gradients before each optimizer step. averaging over disjoint shards is the same arithmetic as one large batch, so the result matches single-process training. python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo from everyo.distributed import average_gradients, shard_indices, spawn def worker(group, inputs, targets): model = build_model() # same seeds on every rank optimizer = eo.sgd(model.parameters(), lr=0.05) rows = shard_indices(len(inputs), group.rank, group.world_size) xs, ys = eo.tensor(inputs[rows]), eo.tensor(targets[rows]) for _ in range(steps): optimizer.zero_grad() residual = model(xs) - ys eo.mean(residual * residual).backward() average_gradients(model.parameters(), group) optimizer.step() return [p.data for p in model.parameters()] results = spawn(worker, world_size=4, args=(inputs, targets)) measured on a 4-core container, 60 steps of a three-layer mlp over 4096 samples: reference / output copy code workers rows/worker seconds max drift ranks agree -------------------------------------------------------------- 1 4096 0.26 0.00e+00 true 2 2048 0.16 1.79e-07 true 4 1024 0.14 2.09e-07 true max drift is the largest weight difference from single-process training; ranks agree says whether every worker ended bit-identical. the pieces component purpose spawn(fn, world_size, args=...) launches the workers, returns their results ordered by rank processgroup passed to each worker: rank , world_size , is_main , barrier() , all_reduce_mean() , broadcast() average_gradients(params, group) the one line a training loop adds shard_indices(count, rank, world_size) splits a dataset; drop_last=true keeps every rank's batch the same size available_workers() usable core count, respecting cgroup and affinity limits workers are processes, not threads: numpy releases the gil for large array operations but holds it for the graph bookkeeping around them, so threads would contend on exactly the part of training that is pure python. the collective itself flattens every gradient into one vector, stages it in a posix shared-memory buffer with one slot per rank, and takes two barriers — one after the write, one after the read, so nobody overwrites a slot a peer is still reading. reduction happens in float64 so summing float32 gradients across workers does not lose a bit to the reduction. two caveats, both load-bearing one machine only. there is no multi-node support, no tcp rendezvous and no nccl. workers communicate through shared memory, which means they share hardware. anything else would be untested. set omp_num_threads=1 before launching python. numpy's blas starts a thread pool per process. on the same 4-core box, four workers took 1.8s with the variable set and 11.0s without — slower than not parallelising at all, because sixteen threads were fighting over four cores. the variable has to be set before numpy loads its backend, so everyo warns rather than pretending it can fix it for you. if spawn times out, the usual cause is a rank that took a different path through the loop: every rank must call the same collectives the same number of times, or the ones that reached the barrier wait forever for the one that did not."},{"id":"running-the-examples","href":"docs-scaling.html#running-the-examples","title":"Running the examples","guide":"Scaling & export","excerpt":"Understand mixed precision, GradScaler, ONNX export, CPU data parallelism, and experimental TCP process groups with their limits.","text":"scaling & export running the examples terminal copy code python examples/mixed_precision.py python examples/onnx_export.py omp_num_threads=1 python examples/distributed_training.py"},{"id":"multi-node-tcp-process-groups","href":"docs-scaling.html#multi-node-tcp-process-groups","title":"Multi-node TCP process groups","guide":"Scaling & export","excerpt":"everyo.distributed_tcp extends gradient collectives beyond one machine. Start a TCPRendezvousServer on rank zero, then call init_tcp_process_group() on every worker with a unique rank. The returned group's all_reduce_mean() is compatible with average_gradients() .","text":"scaling & export multi-node tcp process groups everyo.distributed_tcp extends gradient collectives beyond one machine. start a tcprendezvousserver on rank zero, then call init_tcp_process_group() on every worker with a unique rank. the returned group's all_reduce_mean() is compatible with average_gradients() . the initial backend is cpu and synchronous. it is intended for trusted private networks and correctness-oriented experiments; traffic is not encrypted and there is no nccl/rdma acceleration. put it behind a vpn or private cluster network, never expose the rendezvous port publicly."},{"id":"reading-this-guide","href":"docs-cuda.html#reading-this-guide","title":"Know where computation runs.","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"Start with a working CPU installation. Add the native extension only when compatible NVIDIA hardware and the build toolchain are available.","text":"cuda & devices before you begin know where computation runs. start with a working cpu installation. add the native extension only when compatible nvidia hardware and the build toolchain are available. a device tag is not a resident training tensor the standard tensor(device=\"cuda\") path dispatches supported operations while keeping host-backed tensor data. the separate resident-buffer api uploads data once for supported inference operations. it has no autograd nodes. neither path has gpu runtime validation in project ci."},{"id":"status","href":"docs-cuda.html#status","title":"Status","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"Build the optional CUDA extension, understand fallback and strict mode, inspect kernels, and distinguish host-backed tensors from resident buffers.","text":"cuda & devices status component state kernel sources ( cuda/src ) available pybind11 bindings available cmake build available automatic cpu fallback available kernels used by tensors on a cuda device available (add, multiply, matmul, relu, sum) gpu-resident inference tensors (no per-call transfer) available fused kernels, mixed precision planned"},{"id":"checking-availability","href":"docs-cuda.html#checking-availability","title":"Checking availability","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"import everyo as eo eo.cuda.is_available() # True only with a built extension AND a device eo.cuda.device_count() eo.cuda.unavailable_reason() # a sentence explaining why, or None eo.cuda.runtime_info() # full details, including device properties None of these raise. On a CPU-only machine everyo doctor prints the reason.","text":"cuda & devices checking availability python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo eo.cuda.is_available() # true only with a built extension and a device eo.cuda.device_count() eo.cuda.unavailable_reason() # a sentence explaining why, or none eo.cuda.runtime_info() # full details, including device properties none of these raise. on a cpu-only machine everyo doctor prints the reason."},{"id":"building-the-extension","href":"docs-cuda.html#building-the-extension","title":"Building the extension","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"Requirements: an NVIDIA GPU, the CUDA Toolkit (nvcc), CMake 3.18+ and pybind11. CUDA is supported only on NVIDIA's own supported platforms and toolchains — there is no CUDA path on machines without NVIDIA hardware, and EveryO does not pretend otherwise.","text":"cuda & devices building the extension requirements: an nvidia gpu, the cuda toolkit (nvcc), cmake 3.18+ and pybind11. cuda is supported only on nvidia's own supported platforms and toolchains — there is no cuda path on machines without nvidia hardware, and everyo does not pretend otherwise. terminal copy code pip install -e \".[cuda]\" # installs pybind11 ./scripts/build_cuda.sh or with cmake directly: terminal copy code cmake -s cuda -b build/cuda -dcmake_build_type=release cmake --build build/cuda --parallel the build copies _everyo_cuda*.so next to the python package so import everyo._everyo_cuda resolves. a build host without a gpu is fine: the kernels compile and the extension imports, everyo simply reports cuda as unavailable until it runs on a machine with a device. scripts/build_cuda.sh says so explicitly rather than treating it as a failure. to target a specific compute capability: terminal copy code ./scripts/build_cuda.sh --arch=86 clean up with ./scripts/build_cuda.sh --clean ."},{"id":"using-it","href":"docs-cuda.html#using-it","title":"Using it","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"import everyo as eo x = eo.tensor([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]], device=\"cuda\") y = eo.tensor([[5.0, 6.0], [7.0, 8.0]], device=\"cuda\") z = eo.matmul(x, y) # runs the tiled CUDA kernel when available eo.device(\"auto\") picks CUDA when it is usable and CPU otherwise.","text":"cuda & devices using it python · reference snippet copy code import everyo as eo x = eo.tensor([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]], device=\"cuda\") y = eo.tensor([[5.0, 6.0], [7.0, 8.0]], device=\"cuda\") z = eo.matmul(x, y) # runs the tiled cuda kernel when available eo.device(\"auto\") picks cuda when it is usable and cpu otherwise."},{"id":"fallback-rules","href":"docs-cuda.html#fallback-rules","title":"Fallback rules","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"Core operations select an OperatorBackend through everyo.backends.backend_for() . A dispatch returns the requested backend, the backend that actually executed, and a reason whenever CUDA falls back. This makes unsupported dtypes, ranks, and broadcast shapes testable instead of silently hiding the decision. Host/device conversion is represented by the backend's from_host() and to_host() boundary; the current native extension still performs a transfer for each kernel call.","text":"cuda & devices fallback rules core operations select an operatorbackend through everyo.backends.backend_for() . a dispatch returns the requested backend, the backend that actually executed, and a reason whenever cuda falls back. this makes unsupported dtypes, ranks, and broadcast shapes testable instead of silently hiding the decision. host/device conversion is represented by the backend's from_host() and to_host() boundary; the current native extension still performs a transfer for each kernel call. situation behaviour eo.device(\"cuda\") , no cuda returns the cpu device, logs a warning eo.device(\"cuda\", strict=true) , no cuda raises everyocudaerror eo.tensor(..., device=\"cuda\") , no cuda tensor is created on the cpu eo.cuda.relu(x) , no cuda computes with numpy eo.cuda.relu(x, strict=true) , no cuda raises everyocudaerror everyo_disable_cuda=1 cuda is treated as unavailable even when built nothing crashes because cuda is missing. the only way to get an error is to ask for one with strict=true ."},{"id":"the-kernels","href":"docs-cuda.html#the-kernels","title":"The kernels","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"All of them use EVERYO_CUDA_CHECK , which turns a CUDA error code into a C++ exception carrying the file, line and failing call. EVERYO_CUDA_CHECK_KERNEL also synchronises, so an asynchronous kernel fault surfaces at the launch site instead of at some unrelated call later. Device memory is owned by an RAII DeviceBuffer , so it is released even when a later call throws.","text":"cuda & devices the kernels source kernel notes vector_add.cu vectoradd , vectormultiply one thread per element, bounds-checked relu.cu relu one thread per element matmul.cu matmul 16x16 shared-memory tiling reduction.cu sum block-level tree reduction, host-side final sum device.cu device queries never raises when no driver is present all of them use everyo_cuda_check , which turns a cuda error code into a c++ exception carrying the file, line and failing call. everyo_cuda_check_kernel also synchronises, so an asynchronous kernel fault surfaces at the launch site instead of at some unrelated call later. device memory is owned by an raii devicebuffer , so it is released even when a later call throws."},{"id":"performance","href":"docs-cuda.html#performance","title":"Performance","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"Do not assume the GPU is faster. Each kernel call copies its inputs to the device and the result back; for small problems that transfer dominates and the CPU wins. Measure on your own hardware:","text":"cuda & devices performance do not assume the gpu is faster. each kernel call copies its inputs to the device and the result back; for small problems that transfer dominates and the cpu wins. measure on your own hardware: terminal copy code python benchmarks/benchmark_matmul.py --sizes 128 512 1024 2048 removing per-call transfers by keeping tensors resident on the device is the main optimisation for chained supported operations. use eo.cuda.to_device() to upload float32 data once; addition, multiplication, relu, 2-d matrix multiplication and sum then use device-owned buffers. call .numpy() for an explicit download. the resident api is inference-only and does not yet attach everyo autograd nodes."},{"id":"verifying-correctness","href":"docs-cuda.html#verifying-correctness","title":"Verifying correctness","guide":"CUDA & devices","excerpt":"Kernel output is compared against the NumPy backend in tests/test_devices.py::TestCudaKernels . Those tests skip automatically without a GPU, and run for real with one:","text":"cuda & devices verifying correctness kernel output is compared against the numpy backend in tests/test_devices.py::testcudakernels . those tests skip automatically without a gpu, and run for real with one: terminal copy code pytest tests/test_devices.py -v"},{"id":"reading-this-guide","href":"docs-troubleshooting.html#reading-this-guide","title":"Diagnose one boundary at a time.","guide":"Troubleshooting","excerpt":"Start with the interpreter and environment, then the numerical inputs, then the optional backend. Keep the smallest example that reproduces the problem.","text":"troubleshooting before you begin diagnose one boundary at a time. start with the interpreter and environment, then the numerical inputs, then the optional backend. keep the smallest example that reproduces the problem. environment: activate the intended virtual environment and install with that interpreter. core numerics: run everyo doctor before diagnosing a larger model. inputs: inspect shapes, dtypes, labels, and the loss contract. optional components: test the cpu baseline before adding cuda, tensorflow, or onnx. report: reduce the failing case and include versions and commands without private data."},{"id":"verify-the-environment","href":"docs-troubleshooting.html#verify-the-environment","title":"Verify the environment","guide":"Troubleshooting","excerpt":"python -m pip install -e . everyo info everyo doctor info reports versions and available backends. doctor also performs a core gradient check. Missing TensorFlow or CUDA is expected in a CPU installation. An optional backend being detected does not establish its numerical correctness on your hardware; run the relevant tests before relying on it.","text":"troubleshooting verify the environment terminal copy code python -m pip install -e . everyo info everyo doctor info reports versions and available backends. doctor also performs a core gradient check. missing tensorflow or cuda is expected in a cpu installation. an optional backend being detected does not establish its numerical correctness on your hardware; run the relevant tests before relying on it."},{"id":"common-problems","href":"docs-troubleshooting.html#common-problems","title":"Common problems","guide":"Troubleshooting","excerpt":"Diagnose installation, interpreter selection, missing optional backends, shape errors, JSON reports, and reproducible bug reports.","text":"troubleshooting common problems symptom next step everyo command not found activate the environment and repeat the editable installation. pytest is unavailable install development dependencies with python -m pip install -e \".[dev]\" . cuda is unavailable follow cuda setup ; the core cpu path does not require the native extension. onnx import fails install python -m pip install -e \".[onnx]\" in the active environment. charts cannot open a window save charts to files; headless execution uses the agg backend. distributed example runs slowly limit blas worker threads as described in the example catalog ."},{"id":"machine-readable-reports","href":"docs-troubleshooting.html#machine-readable-reports","title":"Machine-readable reports","guide":"Troubleshooting","excerpt":"everyo info --json everyo benchmark --sizes 32 --repeats 1 --json --output artifacts/benchmark.json With --json , benchmark stdout contains only the JSON result. When --output is also supplied, the file confirmation goes to stderr so stdout can be piped to another program. Timing results depend on hardware, load, and backend; retain the environment report alongside measurements.","text":"troubleshooting machine-readable reports terminal copy code everyo info --json everyo benchmark --sizes 32 --repeats 1 --json --output artifacts/benchmark.json with --json , benchmark stdout contains only the json result. when --output is also supplied, the file confirmation goes to stderr so stdout can be piped to another program. timing results depend on hardware, load, and backend; retain the environment report alongside measurements."},{"id":"report-a-reproducible-problem","href":"docs-troubleshooting.html#report-a-reproducible-problem","title":"Report a reproducible problem","guide":"Troubleshooting","excerpt":"Include the EveryO version or commit, operating system, command, traceback, and a small example using generated data. Include relevant everyo info output after reviewing it for information you do not want to share publicly. Use Support for help and Security for private vulnerability reporting.","text":"troubleshooting report a reproducible problem include the everyo version or commit, operating system, command, traceback, and a small example using generated data. include relevant everyo info output after reviewing it for information you do not want to share publicly. use support for help and security for private vulnerability reporting."}];
